{"kind":"report","text":"Approved: exact head 7bb7060a1e4a6e990716cc63f4620e78f8817708, tree dbc58ed4eeed6892477e83c11ef45aa7ab1f6a34, on builder/ls3-transport-observation-20261115, its four LS-3 artifacts, for request 299787f3 under my promise ca5f83ea and standing da772676. This verdict names the one implementation: LS-3 (request 0139796f, promise b4fe932b, report 880cc2b2, the LS-3 note, primary). `gs review --prepare` resolved that binding and report at this head, with the request closing on the sealed receipt. Main 9e42735a and the approved CE-2 head bc147b4c are both ancestors. CE-2 and LW-1 have not landed. This verdict covers only the four LS-3 paths, and the LS-3 landing follows the CE-2 landing.\n\nWhat I read:\n\n- `scripts/celld-smoke.mjs`. When an install fails with `transport-no-answer`, it maps the CLI's own first line to one of six kinds: `no-reply`, `reply-not-answer`, `reply-too-large`, `deadline`, `missing-fetch` or `unclassified`. It also maps any status to a class such as `5xx`. `celldSmokeTransport` returns the pair only when both values are in their fixed sets. No CLI text crosses.\n- `scripts/test-celld-proxy.mjs`, the observation. `proxyFailureObservation` keeps only fixed values: phases, which may only move forward, the first failure site, an error class taken from a fixed code map or the error type, the proxy decision, the upstream and downstream ends, and the status classes. It arms once, on the smoke's first `POST /v1/scopes`, and keeps the first failure. The proxy now records why it refused admission. A 502 that the proxy writes after an upstream error records `upstream-request`. A relayed upstream reply records `upstream-response-stream` or a complete end, so the two cases stay apart. The client transport records request construction, the TLS request, the headers, the body adapter, the stream and response construction separately. `startupHealth` reports only the probe that completed during startup. `snapshot` gives one `faultBoundary` and one owner, `clientadapter`, `proxy` or `celldbackend`. That names the side to investigate, not a cause.\n- `scripts/test-celld-proxy.mjs`, the output. `failureMetadata` no longer prints the scratch directory path. The observation is captured in the `catch`, before teardown. Only the `celldbackend` owner adds `backendOutput`: the last 12 lines of the backend's stdout and stderr, each at most 512 UTF-8 bytes, with flags for truncation and end of stream. The tails are captured again after the backend stops. Every other owner and every success receipt gets no tail. The full streams stay in private scratch files with mode 0600. A failure while collecting output now stops the child and both pipes, keeps the first error, and still writes the private tail file.\n- `scripts/celld-smoke.test.mjs`. It covers a proxy 502 against a relayed backend reply, sentinels in every dynamic field, the tail limits, a tail for the backend owner only, a getter that throws, and five planned-CLI replies through the real CLI with TEST signing. Those five cases send one request each and leave the planned founding and register unchanged.\n- The LS-3 note. It states that the cause is still open, that no Worker, SDK, fetch shim, runtime pin, trust or retry policy changes, and that the control kept no raw TAP files.\n\nWhat I checked:\n\n- Scope. The four artifacts name the head: the note and the three scripts. From the gated head ffea78bb to this head only the LS-3 note and the sprint report change.\n- Gate, END 3a019d16, accepted by ruling 31396da9. `/Users/hughpyle/play/artroom-evidence/2026-11-15-ls3-full-gate-attempt1/gate.log` has SHA-256 8c28c3da…, and `result.json` has 6e190f72…, as the END states. The log names head ffea78bb and tree a8d1f5ab with 0 changed files. Kernel, Node 205, Counting 56, Plain 4877 with 1 skipped and Native 1136 with 2 skipped passed, and its last line is \"gate passed\". `result.json` keeps `verificationExit` 2 and status `FAIL`, because the wrapper's \"Main moved\" check fired when the sprint report landed. I treat the gate as passed under the planner's ruling, and the wrapper result stays as recorded. Scale selected nothing.\n- Cheap checks. The commit-message check passes: 15 checked, 0 flagged. `git diff --check` from bc147b4c is clean. I ran no tests.\n\nArchitecture: sound. The classification stays in the scripts, as ruling cdadb4c1 set. One observation object records fixed values at each seam, and one function chooses the owner from them. The smoke and the proxy share the status-class helper.\n\nSecurity: no blocking finding.\n\n- Every new public field takes a value from a fixed set. The tests put sentinels into each dynamic input, and none of them reaches the output.\n- The scratch path left the public error line.\n- The backend tail is the one approved exception, and it prints raw child text. The \"Local Worker smoke\" workflow runs `scripts/test-celld-proxy.mjs`, and this repository is public, so a backend failure prints up to 24 lines of celld output into a public Actions log. The run's secrets are generated TEST values for that run. Before the tail first prints, check that no tool in the celld backend echoes a `.dev.vars` value or the operator secret. If one does, the exception prints that run's TEST secret.\n\nSimplification: no required change.\n\nQualifications that stand: the cause of the Linux failure is still open. The five CLI cases use injected replies, so no real celld, TLS or Worker result follows. The collector repair has a source review and no runtime witness. The privacy control kept no raw TAP files. No workflow pass, deployment or release follows. This approval authorizes no push, landing or deployment. The planner decides the landing, after CE-2.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:880cc2b2277a05eb39cf44f0c2300577e7acce25","binding":"assigned","head":"7bb7060a1e4a6e990716cc63f4620e78f8817708","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d